\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{Prerequisites and conventions}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The notes address two readers. One is a mathematician who wants the
neuron models as worked examples of the statements. The other is a
neuroscientist who wants the statements behind phase-plane methods.}

\p{The notes assume calculus of several variables, linear algebra through
eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and the solutions of first-order ordinary
differential equations. The development of those subjects stays in the
books cited here. A derivative of a coordinate along a solution is
the calculus the notes use. An eigenvalue, an eigenvector, and the solutions of
#{\dot x=Ax} for a #{2\times 2} matrix #{A} are the linear algebra.
The time set of an ordinary differential equation is an interval, and
a solution is a differentiable map on that interval. The notes also
assume the words membrane potential and ionic current.
In \ref{fnip-0003}, #{V} is called the membrane potential. In the
voltage equation of \ref{fnip-0005}, the ionic current is the sum of
the conductance terms.
\citet{Chapter 2, p. 25}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
is an account of the ions and the membrane currents, beginning with
sodium, potassium, calcium, and chloride. That chapter says at its
opening that a reader who already knows the Hodgkin–Huxley formalism
can pass over it, and it points to Dayan and Abbott for a fuller
account.
The derivative in a [trajectory](fnip-0009) is the first use of the
calculus of several variables.
\citet{Theorem 1.0.1, §1.0, p. 3}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
is the existence and uniqueness theorem the notes rely on for that
trajectory, and
\citet{§1.1, p. 8}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
is the linear system #{ \dot x = Ax }.
The [dynamical system](fnip-0004) is the first use of a state space.
\citet{Chapter 1, p. 1}{kuznetsov2004elements}
introduces that state space, and
\citet{§1.2, pp. 8–10}{kuznetsov2004elements}
introduces orbits and phase portraits.
The drawing in \ref{fnip-0011} is the first
use of the phase plane.
\citet{§3.3, p. 51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
opens that phase plane for the Morris–Lecar pair.}

\p{The standing conventions are these. A state is a point #{x} of
#{\mathbb{R}^{n}}, time is continuous, and the evolution is
#{\dot x=f(x)} for a vector field #{f} that is smooth on the domain
named in its statement. The flow is the family #{\varphi^{t}}, also
written #{\varphi_{t}}, as in
[Dynamical system](fnip-0004). A statement names its state space, its
vector field and its parameters before it uses them, and an argument
that uses coordinates first names them, in order; the running
Morris–Lecar coordinates are the book's pair #{(V,n)}, as in
\ref{fnip-0005}. A parameter value, and the unit in which it is printed,
are those of the book that the example cites, and an example names once
the column of \citet{Table 3.1, p. 51}{ermentrout2010mathematical} from
which its parameters come.}

\p{A reader should be able to compute the eigenvalues of a #{2\times 2}
matrix and to sketch the solutions of a linear system in the plane.}
